§§ `Rules 2 to 4 -- reference of questions of foreign law to foreign courts`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- src:4413 | 2. —(1) Where in any proceedings before the Court there arises any question relating to the law of any specified foreign country or to the application of such law, the Court may, on the application of one or more of the parties, order that proceedings be commenced in a specified court in that specified foreign country seeking a determination of such question. (2) An application for an order under paragraph (1) must be made by summons and supported by an affidavit stating the grounds for the application.
@ref O 29 r 2(1) (src:4413)
GIVEN `the proceedings are before the Court` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a question relating to the law of a specified foreign country or to the application of such law arises` IS A BOOLEAN
      `one or more of the parties apply` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the order is that proceedings be commenced in a specified court in that specified foreign country seeking a determination of the question` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 29 r 2(1) the Court may order proceedings in a specified court in a specified foreign country` MEANS
    IF `the proceedings are before the Court` AND `a question relating to the law of a specified foreign country or to the application of such law arises`
    THEN IF `one or more of the parties apply` AND `the order is that proceedings be commenced in a specified court in that specified foreign country seeking a determination of the question`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "Where in any proceedings before the Court there arises any question relating to the law of any specified foreign country or to the application of such law, the Court may, on the application of one or more of the parties, order that proceedings be commenced in a specified court in that specified foreign country seeking a determination of such question"
    ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where in any proceedings before the Court there arises any question relating to the law of any specified foreign country or to the application of such law, the Court may, on the application of one or more of the parties, order that proceedings be commenced in a specified court in that specified foreign country seeking a determination of such question"
-- src:4413 | 2. —(1) Where in any proceedings before the Court there arises any question relating to the law of any specified foreign country or to the application of such law, the Court may, on the application of one or more of the parties, order that proceedings be commenced in a specified court in that specified foreign country seeking a determination of such question. (2) An application for an order under paragraph (1) must be made by summons and supported by an affidavit stating the grounds for the application.
@ref O 29 r 2(2) (src:4413)
GIVEN `the application is for an order under paragraph (1)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the application is made by summons` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is supported by an affidavit stating the grounds for the application` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 29 r 2(2) the application is made by summons and supported by an affidavit` MEANS
    IF `the application is for an order under paragraph (1)`
    THEN IF `the application is made by summons` AND `it is supported by an affidavit stating the grounds for the application`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (a requirement not met; O 3 r 2(1) makes every requirement subject to the Court's discretion, so the text does not itself say no): "An application for an order under paragraph (1) must be made by summons and supported by an affidavit stating the grounds for the application" (a bare must not done; open convention question QB)
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the trigger is unmet, so the duty does not arise; `o 29 r 2(2) the application is made by summons and supported by an affidavit (the duty arises)` says so (open convention question QD)
@ref O 29 r 2(2) (src:4413)
GIVEN `the application is for an order under paragraph (1)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 29 r 2(2) the application is made by summons and supported by an affidavit (the duty arises)` MEANS
    IF `the application is for an order under paragraph (1)`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "An application for an order under paragraph (1) must be made by summons and supported by an affidavit stating the grounds for the application"
-- src:4414 | (3) The Court may give such directions as the Court thinks fit for the preparation of a statement of the issue from which the question arises for inclusion with the question of law to be determined by the specified court in the specified foreign country.
@ref O 29 r 2(3) (src:4414)
GIVEN `the directions are for the preparation of a statement of the issue from which the question arises, for inclusion with the question of law to be determined by the specified court in the specified foreign country` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 29 r 2(3) the Court may give directions for a statement of the issue` MEANS
    IF `the directions are for the preparation of a statement of the issue from which the question arises, for inclusion with the question of law to be determined by the specified court in the specified foreign country`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The Court may give such directions as the Court thinks fit for the preparation of a statement of the issue from which the question arises for inclusion with the question of law to be determined by the specified court in the specified foreign country"
-- src:4416 | 3. Nothing in this Order prevents the Court from ordering, on its own motion, that proceedings be commenced in any court of competent jurisdiction in any foreign country (not being a specified foreign country) seeking a determination of any question relating to the law of that foreign country or to the application of such law.
@ref O 29 r 3 (src:4416)
GIVEN `the order is made on the Court's own motion` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the proceedings are to be commenced in a court of competent jurisdiction in a foreign country` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the foreign country is not a specified foreign country` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it seeks a determination of a question relating to the law of that foreign country or to the application of such law` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 29 r 3 this Order does not prevent the Court ordering proceedings in another foreign country on its own motion` MEANS
    IF `the order is made on the Court's own motion` AND `the proceedings are to be commenced in a court of competent jurisdiction in a foreign country` AND `the foreign country is not a specified foreign country` AND `it seeks a determination of a question relating to the law of that foreign country or to the application of such law`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "Nothing in this Order prevents the Court from ordering, on its own motion, that proceedings be commenced in any court of competent jurisdiction in any foreign country (not being a specified foreign country) seeking a determination of any question relating to the law of that foreign country or to the application of such law"
-- src:4418 | 4. An order made by the Court under Rule 2 or 3 must — ( a )
-- src:4419 | state the question that is to be determined in relation to the law of the foreign country;
-- src:4420 | ( b )
-- src:4421 | state the facts or assumptions upon which the question is to be determined;
-- src:4422 | ( c )
-- src:4423 | contain a statement to the effect that the court in the foreign country may vary the facts or assumptions and the question to be determined; and
-- src:4424 | ( d )
-- src:4425 | state whether and to what extent the parties may depart from the facts or assumptions in the determination of the question by the court of the foreign country.
@ref O 29 r 4 (src:4418-4425)
GIVEN `the order is made by the Court under Rule 2 or 3` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it states the question that is to be determined in relation to the law of the foreign country` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it states the facts or assumptions upon which the question is to be determined` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it contains a statement to the effect that the court in the foreign country may vary the facts or assumptions and the question to be determined` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it states whether and to what extent the parties may depart from the facts or assumptions in the determination of the question by the court of the foreign country` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 29 r 4 the order states what it must state` MEANS
    IF `the order is made by the Court under Rule 2 or 3`
    THEN IF `it states the question that is to be determined in relation to the law of the foreign country` AND `it states the facts or assumptions upon which the question is to be determined` AND `it contains a statement to the effect that the court in the foreign country may vary the facts or assumptions and the question to be determined` AND `it states whether and to what extent the parties may depart from the facts or assumptions in the determination of the question by the court of the foreign country`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (a requirement not met; O 3 r 2(1) makes every requirement subject to the Court's discretion, so the text does not itself say no): "An order made by the Court under Rule 2 or 3 must — state the question that is to be determined in relation to the law of the foreign country; state the facts or assumptions upon which the question is to be determined; contain a statement to the effect that the court in the foreign country may vary the facts or assumptions and the question to be determined; and state whether and to what extent the parties may depart from the facts or assumptions" (a bare must not done; open convention question QB)
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the trigger is unmet, so the duty does not arise; `o 29 r 4 the order states what it must state (the duty arises)` says so (open convention question QD)
@ref O 29 r 4 (src:4418-4425)
GIVEN `the order is made by the Court under Rule 2 or 3` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 29 r 4 the order states what it must state (the duty arises)` MEANS
    IF `the order is made by the Court under Rule 2 or 3`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "An order made by the Court under Rule 2 or 3 must — state the question that is to be determined in relation to the law of the foreign country; state the facts or assumptions upon which the question is to be determined; contain a statement to the effect that the court in the foreign country may vary the facts or assumptions and the question to be determined; and state whether and to what extent the parties may depart from the facts or assumptions"

